    answer: '<p>Our top recommendations:</p><ul><li><strong>Authy:</strong> Best overall—cloud backup, multi-device sync, easy recovery</li><li><strong>1Password:</strong> Best if you already use it for passwords—built-in authenticator</li><li><strong>Google Authenticator:</strong> Simple and reliable, but no cloud backup</li><li><strong>Microsoft Authenticator:</strong> Great for Microsoft/Azure environments</li></ul><p>Avoid SMS-based 2FA when possible—it\'s vulnerable to SIM swapping attacks.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I lose my phone?',
    answer: '<p>This is why backup codes are critical. If you lose your phone:</p><ul><li><strong>Use backup codes:</strong> Each code works once to regain access</li><li><strong>Use backup device:</strong> If you use Authy, you can access from another synced device</li><li><strong>Contact support:</strong> Most services have account recovery processes (slow but works)</li><li><strong>Hardware key backup:</strong> If you have a second security key, use it</li></ul><p>Always store backup codes securely (password manager or printed in a safe place).</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is SMS 2FA better than nothing?',
    answer: '<p><strong>Yes, SMS is better than no 2FA at all.</strong> However, it\'s the weakest form:</p><ul><li><strong>SIM swapping:</strong> Attackers can convince carriers to transfer your number</li><li><strong>SS7 vulnerabilities:</strong> Technical exploits in phone networks</li><li><strong>Phone theft:</strong> If your phone is stolen and unlocked</li></ul><p>Use SMS 2FA as a stepping stone, but upgrade to authenticator apps or hardware keys for important accounts.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use 2FA on all accounts?',
    answer: '<p>Enable 2FA on all accounts that support it, but prioritize:</p><ul><li><strong>Critical:</strong> Email (it\'s your recovery point), banking, password manager</li><li><strong>High priority:</strong> Work accounts, cloud storage, social media</li><li><strong>Good to have:</strong> Shopping sites, forums, any account with payment info</li></ul><p>Your email is especially important—if someone compromises your email, they can reset passwords on other accounts.</p>'
